1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,700
Twice a year, most of America
moves its clocks by one hour.

2
00:00:04,277 --> 00:00:06,377
In March, we lose an hour of sleep.

3
00:00:07,037 --> 00:00:09,037
In November, the hour comes back.

4
00:00:09,117 --> 00:00:11,937
And every time, the same argument starts.

5
00:00:12,697 --> 00:00:14,297
It's bad for our health.

6
00:00:14,674 --> 00:00:17,554
It's pointless.
Just pick one time and keep it.

7
00:00:17,962 --> 00:00:19,922
Politicians seem to agree.

8
00:00:20,302 --> 00:00:25,462
In 2022, the United States Senate
passed a bill to stop changing the clocks.

9
00:00:25,542 --> 00:00:27,902
Without a single objection.

10
00:00:28,643 --> 00:00:32,523
It went nowhere. Four years later,
the House passed another bill,

11
00:00:32,753 --> 00:00:35,043
to make daylight saving time permanent.

12
00:00:35,663 --> 00:00:37,503
It's now stuck in the Senate.

13
00:00:38,063 --> 00:00:39,803
And the clocks are still changing.

14
00:00:40,457 --> 00:00:43,597
So if changing the clocks
is such a terrible idea,

15
00:00:43,777 --> 00:00:45,877
why is it so difficult to stop?

16
00:00:47,063 --> 00:00:49,103
America has actually tried.

17
00:00:49,723 --> 00:00:52,783
In 1974, it stopped changing the clocks.

18
00:00:53,563 --> 00:00:55,803
The experiment didn't last a year.

19
00:00:56,357 --> 00:01:00,317
To see why, we need to start with the
problem the clocks were trying to solve.

20
00:01:02,917 --> 00:01:06,917
The problem is simple.
Daylight changes through the year.

21
00:01:07,197 --> 00:01:08,697
Our schedules don't.

22
00:01:09,204 --> 00:01:10,724
School starts at eight.

23
00:01:10,804 --> 00:01:12,724
Work starts at nine.

24
00:01:12,804 --> 00:01:15,464
In June and in December alike.

25
00:01:16,131 --> 00:01:18,171
But the Sun doesn't follow a timetable.

26
00:01:19,171 --> 00:01:23,771
In Seattle, the shortest day of the year
has about eight and a half hours of light.

27
00:01:24,451 --> 00:01:25,891
The longest has sixteen.

28
00:01:26,705 --> 00:01:29,125
In Miami, the difference is much smaller.

29
00:01:29,845 --> 00:01:33,405
Ten and a half hours in winter,
not quite fourteen in summer.

30
00:01:34,057 --> 00:01:37,257
The farther north you live,
the more the day stretches and shrinks

31
00:01:37,487 --> 00:01:39,377
around a schedule that never moves.

32
00:01:40,283 --> 00:01:42,443
In summer, that means something odd.

33
00:01:43,143 --> 00:01:47,583
On standard time, the Sun in Boston
rises just after four in the morning.

34
00:01:48,203 --> 00:01:51,543
Hours of daylight,
while almost everyone is asleep.

35
00:01:52,181 --> 00:01:54,961
Daylight saving time is a trick
for moving some of that light

36
00:01:55,191 --> 00:01:57,241
from the early morning to the evening.

37
00:01:57,541 --> 00:01:59,461
Not by changing the Sun.

38
00:01:59,541 --> 00:02:02,381
By changing what we call the hour.

39
00:02:02,969 --> 00:02:06,449
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

40
00:02:07,069 --> 00:02:08,689
It really helps the channel.

41
00:02:11,059 --> 00:02:14,279
And as a national law,
it started as a war measure.

42
00:02:14,931 --> 00:02:20,771
On 30 April 1916, Germany moved its clocks
forward to save coal for the war.

43
00:02:21,451 --> 00:02:23,671
Britain followed three weeks later.

44
00:02:24,266 --> 00:02:28,786
The United States joined in 1918,
and gave it up a year later.

45
00:02:29,626 --> 00:02:31,306
It was so unpopular with farmers

46
00:02:31,536 --> 00:02:34,526
that Congress repealed it
over the President's veto.

47
00:02:35,244 --> 00:02:39,984
In the Second World War it came back,
all year round, under the name "War Time".

48
00:02:40,609 --> 00:02:44,229
After the war, every state and city
did more or less what it wanted.

49
00:02:45,269 --> 00:02:49,129
In the 1960s, one bus ride,
just thirty-five miles

50
00:02:49,359 --> 00:02:53,729
from Ohio into West Virginia,
went through seven changes of time.

51
00:02:54,318 --> 00:02:59,398
In 1966, Congress finally set
one set of rules for the whole country.

52
00:03:00,098 --> 00:03:04,238
States could opt out entirely,
but they couldn't pick their own dates.

53
00:03:04,697 --> 00:03:08,497
And so a wartime measure
quietly became part of normal life.

54
00:03:11,257 --> 00:03:12,917
The cost is real, though.

55
00:03:13,157 --> 00:03:17,737
Our bodies run on an internal clock,
and that clock is set mostly by light.

56
00:03:18,181 --> 00:03:20,221
When the clocks jump forward in March,

57
00:03:20,441 --> 00:03:24,161
the alarm rings an hour earlier,
as far as our body is concerned.

58
00:03:25,081 --> 00:03:27,281
For days, many people sleep worse.

59
00:03:27,964 --> 00:03:29,424
Some studies have found a brief rise

60
00:03:29,654 --> 00:03:33,244
in car crashes and heart attacks
right after that change.

61
00:03:35,874 --> 00:03:39,074
So the obvious answer is:
stop changing the clocks.

62
00:03:39,774 --> 00:03:42,014
Pick one time, and keep it all year.

63
00:03:42,564 --> 00:03:44,109
Fine.

64
00:03:44,209 --> 00:03:45,409
But which one?

65
00:03:46,795 --> 00:03:50,015
Permanent standard time
keeps winter mornings as they are.

66
00:03:50,095 --> 00:03:53,835
But every summer evening
loses an hour of light,

67
00:03:53,995 --> 00:03:57,455
and in the north, the Sun rises
around four in the morning.

68
00:03:58,008 --> 00:04:01,008
Permanent daylight saving time
keeps the long summer evenings.

69
00:04:01,238 --> 00:04:04,488
But in winter, the mornings get dark.

70
00:04:04,648 --> 00:04:06,148
Really dark.

71
00:04:06,812 --> 00:04:10,932
In Indianapolis, the Sun would rise
after nine in early January.

72
00:04:11,712 --> 00:04:14,172
In Detroit and Seattle, around nine.

73
00:04:14,503 --> 00:04:17,683
And America knows what that feels like,
because it tried.

74
00:04:18,218 --> 00:04:22,238
In January 1974,
in the middle of an oil crisis,

75
00:04:22,658 --> 00:04:24,618
the United States switched to
daylight saving

76
00:04:24,848 --> 00:04:27,758
time all year round, to save energy.

77
00:04:28,226 --> 00:04:32,466
When the law was signed,
about four in five Americans supported it.

78
00:04:33,206 --> 00:04:37,766
Then came the dark January mornings, and
children walking to school before sunrise.

79
00:04:38,756 --> 00:04:41,816
Within weeks, support fell
to around two in five.

80
00:04:42,457 --> 00:04:46,337
By October, Congress voted to bring
back standard time for the winter.

81
00:04:47,117 --> 00:04:48,617
It had been planned for two years.

82
00:04:48,847 --> 00:04:52,917
Year-round daylight saving time
lasted ten months.

83
00:04:54,208 --> 00:04:56,008
And here's the strange part.

84
00:04:56,668 --> 00:05:00,288
Today, the law lets any state choose
permanent standard time.

85
00:05:01,028 --> 00:05:03,428
Arizona and Hawaii already have it.

86
00:05:03,715 --> 00:05:05,295
But nineteen states have passed laws

87
00:05:05,525 --> 00:05:08,795
for the other one,
permanent daylight saving time.

88
00:05:09,515 --> 00:05:11,815
And that, they can't have
without Congress.

89
00:05:12,581 --> 00:05:14,861
So people agree that they hate the switch.

90
00:05:15,461 --> 00:05:17,461
They don't agree on what to switch to.

91
00:05:20,041 --> 00:05:22,161
And it's not just an American problem.

92
00:05:22,981 --> 00:05:27,941
In 2011, Russia stopped changing
its clocks and kept summer time all year.

93
00:05:28,523 --> 00:05:32,223
In Moscow, the winter Sun
now rose at around ten in the morning.

94
00:05:32,603 --> 00:05:37,423
Three years later, Russia went
the other way, to permanent winter time.

95
00:05:37,986 --> 00:05:42,506
In 2018, the European Union
asked its citizens what they thought.

96
00:05:42,866 --> 00:05:45,726
More than four and a half
million answered,

97
00:05:45,886 --> 00:05:49,026
and eighty-four per cent of them
wanted the changes to end.

98
00:05:49,668 --> 00:05:51,768
It wasn't a representative poll.

99
00:05:52,648 --> 00:05:54,568
Most of the answers came from Germany.

100
00:05:55,428 --> 00:05:59,348
The result showed how strongly
the people who took part felt about it.

101
00:05:59,901 --> 00:06:03,801
The following spring,
the European Parliament voted to do it.

102
00:06:04,301 --> 00:06:08,361
All that was left was for the countries
to agree which time each would keep.

103
00:06:08,981 --> 00:06:10,481
And that's where it stopped.

104
00:06:11,281 --> 00:06:14,061
Governments worried that neighbours
would choose differently,

105
00:06:14,221 --> 00:06:16,861
and Europe would end up
as a patchwork of time zones.

106
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:19,379
That was in 2019.

107
00:06:19,859 --> 00:06:21,759
Europe is still changing its clocks.

108
00:06:24,539 --> 00:06:26,899
So maybe we're asking the wrong question.

109
00:06:27,419 --> 00:06:30,159
Maybe the clock isn't the thing
we should be moving.

110
00:06:31,423 --> 00:06:34,763
Look at Spain. Madrid lies west of London.

111
00:06:35,263 --> 00:06:38,183
By the Sun, it belongs
in London's time zone.

112
00:06:38,562 --> 00:06:40,862
But in 1940, Spain moved its clocks

113
00:06:41,092 --> 00:06:44,822
to Central European Time,
the time of Paris and Berlin.

114
00:06:45,502 --> 00:06:47,282
And it never moved them back.

115
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:52,240
So in summer, the Sun over Madrid
is highest at around a quarter past two,

116
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:54,580
and it sets close to ten in the evening.

117
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:57,200
But Spanish life stayed with the Sun.

118
00:06:57,780 --> 00:07:01,160
Lunch at two. Dinner at nine, or later.

119
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:03,140
By the clock, that's late.

120
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:05,100
By the Sun, it isn't.

121
00:07:05,589 --> 00:07:09,669
Spain is a reminder that the clock
and the schedule are two different things.

122
00:07:10,249 --> 00:07:12,689
You can move the day
without touching the clock.

123
00:07:13,146 --> 00:07:15,186
And in small ways, we already do.

124
00:07:15,526 --> 00:07:17,146
Summer hours at the office.

125
00:07:17,606 --> 00:07:20,526
Flexible start times. Shift work.

126
00:07:20,919 --> 00:07:22,679
So why not do that everywhere?

127
00:07:23,259 --> 00:07:26,039
Start work at seven in summer,
and at nine in winter,

128
00:07:26,269 --> 00:07:28,179
and never touch the clocks again?

129
00:07:28,961 --> 00:07:33,261
Because a society is not one person
adjusting their alarm clock.

130
00:07:33,642 --> 00:07:37,302
If your office starts earlier,
your children's school has to as well.

131
00:07:37,482 --> 00:07:40,282
And their bus. And the daycare.

132
00:07:40,562 --> 00:07:42,662
And the shop you stop at on the way.

133
00:07:42,879 --> 00:07:44,379
And the train timetable.

134
00:07:44,459 --> 00:07:48,479
And the company in another state
you have a call with every morning.

135
00:07:49,121 --> 00:07:51,921
Every one of those would have
to be moved separately, by someone,

136
00:07:52,151 --> 00:07:54,281
and agreed with everyone else.

137
00:07:54,913 --> 00:07:58,113
Changing the clocks does all
of that in a single step.

138
00:07:58,733 --> 00:08:00,673
At two in the morning on a Sunday,

139
00:08:00,833 --> 00:08:03,633
everyone following the same rules
changes together.

140
00:08:04,355 --> 00:08:06,695
It's clumsy. But it's brutally simple.

141
00:08:08,016 --> 00:08:09,856
Well... simple to coordinate.

142
00:08:10,736 --> 00:08:12,876
Anyone who has written software
that deals with time

143
00:08:13,106 --> 00:08:15,616
knows how much trouble one hour can cause.

144
00:08:16,306 --> 00:08:19,426
In November, the hour from one to two
happens twice.

145
00:08:20,386 --> 00:08:23,466
In March, the hour from two to three
doesn't happen at all.

146
00:08:23,999 --> 00:08:27,199
Calendars, recurring meetings,
alarms, payroll, night shifts...

147
00:08:27,429 --> 00:08:29,979
all of them have to know about it.

148
00:08:30,295 --> 00:08:32,075
And the rules keep changing.

149
00:08:32,695 --> 00:08:34,035
The database that computers use

150
00:08:34,265 --> 00:08:36,975
to keep track of time zones
is updated again and again,

151
00:08:37,205 --> 00:08:40,695
whenever some government, somewhere,
changes its clocks.

152
00:08:43,945 --> 00:08:46,125
So why do we still change the clocks?

153
00:08:47,563 --> 00:08:49,963
Not because anyone thinks it's perfect.

154
00:08:50,063 --> 00:08:53,603
Because daylight changes,
and our schedules don't.

155
00:08:54,083 --> 00:08:57,263
We could change our schedules
instead of changing our clocks.

156
00:08:58,103 --> 00:09:00,123
And sometimes, we already do.

157
00:09:00,709 --> 00:09:03,909
But a society is millions of people,
businesses, schools,

158
00:09:03,989 --> 00:09:07,789
trains and computers,
trying to agree on when the day begins.

159
00:09:08,549 --> 00:09:09,989
Daylight changes.

160
00:09:10,529 --> 00:09:13,009
Our schedules don't
change nearly as easily.

161
00:09:13,681 --> 00:09:17,061
Maybe daylight saving time
isn't a perfect solution.

162
00:09:17,741 --> 00:09:19,141
Maybe there isn't one.

163
00:09:19,510 --> 00:09:24,290
But of all the imperfect ones, switching
twice a year might just be the least bad.

164
00:09:24,897 --> 00:09:27,537
The strange thing isn't
that we change the clocks.

165
00:09:28,257 --> 00:09:31,337
It's that moving one hour on a clock
can be easier than changing

166
00:09:31,567 --> 00:09:33,927
the daily schedule of an entire society.
